Certainly! Here is a simple C++ program that takes voltage and current as input from the user, calculates the power using the formula:

Power (P) = Voltage (V) × Current (I)

and calculates the resistance using Ohm's law:

Resistance (R) = Voltage (V) / Current (I)

The program also checks for division by zero to avoid runtime errors.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    double voltage, current, power, resistance;

    // Input voltage and current
    cout << "Enter voltage (V) in volts: ";
    cin >> voltage;

    cout << "Enter current (I) in amperes: ";
    cin >> current;

    // Check for zero current to avoid division by zero
    if (current == 0) {
        cout << "Current cannot be zero for resistance calculation." << endl;
        // Power can still be calculated if current is zero (will be zero)
    } else {
        // Calculate power and resistance
        power = voltage * current;
        resistance = voltage / current;
    }

    // Display results
    cout << fixed << setprecision(3); // for nice decimal output
    cout << "Power (P) = " << power << " watts" << endl;

    if (current != 0) {
        cout << "Resistance (R) = " << resistance << " ohms" << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### How to Use
1. Copy the code into a file, for example, `electrical_calcs.cpp`.
2. Compile it using a C++ compiler, e.g., `g++`:
   ```
   g++ -o electrical_calcs electrical_calcs.cpp
   ```
3. Run the compiled program:
   ```
   ./electrical_calcs
   ```

### Example Input/Output

```
